      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "The aim of this study was to define the utility of the combined measurement of troponin I, myoglobin, C-reactive protein, fibrinogen, and homocysteine to predict risk in non-ST elevation acute coronary syndromes."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Troponin I, myoglobin, high-sensitivity C-reactive protein, fibrinogen, and homocysteine were measured in 557 consecutive patients admitted to our institution for non-ST elevation acute coronary syndrome. The risk for major events (death or nonfatal myocardial infarction) at first month and at first year follow-up was analyzed."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "In a multivariate model adjusting for baseline characteristics and electrocardiographic changes, the only biomarkers related to major events at first month were C-reactive protein (P = .007) and myoglobin (P = .02), and at first year troponin I (P = .02), C-reactive protein (P = .03), and homocysteine (P = .04). The rate of major events depending on the number (0-5) of elevated biomarkers were at first month: 4.1%, 3.7%, 5.7%, 6.1%, 6.5%, and 30.8% (P < .0001), and at first year: 8.2%, 11.1%, 12.3%, 16.2%, 23.7%, and 50% (P < .0001). A simple score including the number of elevated biomarkers showed an adjusted risk of major events of 1.6 [1.3-1.9] at first month and of 1.4 [1.2-1.7] at first year."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Markers of myocardial damage, inflammation, and homocysteine analyzed separately provide prognostic information. The number of elevated biomarkers is an independent risk predictor of major events."
        }
      ]},
